.TH ALLOW_REGISTER 4 2026-10-06 Lustre "Lustre Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
allow_register \- control registration of new Lustre targets (MDTs/OSTs)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY
.BI "lctl set_param allow_register=" TIMEOUT
.SY
.B lctl get_param allow_register
.YS
.SS PROPERTIES
.TP
.B Access Permissions
.BR 644 " | " -rw-r--r--
.TP
.B Scope
Global (MGS)
.TP
.B Config
MGS only
.TP
.B Initial default
.br
.RB allow_register= 0
(disabled), except for a one-time automatic 24-hour default window opened on
the first target registration on a truly fresh MGS (one with no filesystem
config log of any kind yet) after the modules load; see
.B AUTOMATIC DEFAULT WINDOW
below.
.TP
.B Valid Range
.br
.RB allow_register= 0
.br
.RB allow_register= 168
The maximum timed window is 7 days
.RB ( 168h
/
.BR 604800s );
larger values are rejected with
.BR EINVAL .
Use
.B unlimited
for an open-ended window.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B allow_register
controls whether new Lustre filesystem targets (MDTs and OSTs) can register
with the MGS.
.B TIMEOUT
argument specifies how long registration is permitted.
This parameter provides a security mechanism to limit the window during which
new filesystems or targets can be added to the cluster.
.PP
The parameter supports three types of values:
.TP
.B 0
Registration is disabled. No new targets can register with the MGS.
Existing targets can continue to operate normally.
.TP
.B unlimited
Registration is enabled indefinitely.
New targets can register at any time without restriction. No timer is armed.
.TP
.IR TIMEOUT [ UNITS ]
Registration is enabled for a time-limited window of
.I TIMEOUT
time, with optional
.I UNITS
magnitude (default hours).
When the timeout expires, registration is automatically disabled (set to 0).
A console warning message is logged when the timeout expires.
.PP
Units supported:
.RS
.TP
.B s
seconds
.TP
.B m
minutes
.TP
.B h
hours (default if no units is specified)
.RE
.PP
When a time-limited value is set, a timer is started that automatically
disables registration when it expires.
Reading the parameter with
.B "lctl get_param allow_register"
while a timer is active shows the remaining time with
an explicit
.B s
unit (for example,
.BR 59s ),
so that it can be used directly as input to
.BR "lctl set_param allow_register=59s" .
.SH AUTOMATIC DEFAULT WINDOW
.B allow_register
defaults to
.B 0
(disabled) so that a cluster where the administrator never touches this
parameter is secure by default. To avoid that default also blocking the
very first setup of a brand-new cluster, the MGS automatically opens a
one-time, non-persistent
.B 24-hour
default window the first time a target registers, but
.B only
if the MGS is truly fresh, i.e. it does not yet have a config log for
.B any
filesystem in its
.B CONFIGS
directory. The window opens as if
.B "lctl set_param allow_register=24h"
had been run.
.PP
This restriction applies to the MGS as a whole, not to individual filesystem
names being registered. Once the MGS has a config log for at least one
filesystem, it will never auto-arm again, even if a subsequent registration
involves a different, previously unknown filesystem name
(e.g., after a reboot or an
.B obdclass
reload).
.PP
This automatic window
.B never
re-arms itself: it can fire at most once per module load, regardless of
how many filesystems or targets are subsequently created. It is also
skipped entirely if the administrator has already set
.B allow_register
explicitly (via
.BR "lctl set_param" ,
the MGS
.B params
config log, or the
.B mgs
module's boot-time parameter) before the first target registers.
.PP
Administrators who want registration locked down immediately, even
during initial setup, can still set
.B "lctl set_param allow_register=0"
right after starting the MGS.
.SH PERSISTENCE
Stopping and starting the MGS service alone does
.B not
reset
.B allow_register
while
.B obdclass
remains loaded.
After a full node reboot, or any path that unloads and reloads
.BR obdclass ,
the value returns to its initial default of
.B 0
(subject to the automatic default window described in
.B AUTOMATIC DEFAULT WINDOW
above).
.PP
Steady-state values
.RB ( 0
and
.BR unlimited )
may be written to the MGS
.B params
configuration log with
.BR "lctl set_param -P"
(see
.BR lctl-set_param (8)).
Timed windows cannot be persisted with
.BR -P
(rejected with
.BR EINVAL
by the MGS):
only
.B 0
and
.B unlimited
are allowed.
Replaying a duration would open a new registration window on every mount.
.PP
.B -P
settings are restored whenever a node processes the
.B params
log, which happens on every MDT or OST mount on that node (including a
failover mount), not just once at boot. Each such replay unconditionally
overwrites the live value and cancels any running timer, even one just
opened with
.BR "lctl set_param allow_register=" N .
On a combined MGS/MDT node,
.B "lctl set_param -P allow_register=0"
restores the lockdown policy after reboot, but this also means opening a
temporary window and then mounting another target on the
.B same
node can close the window early, before targets on other nodes register
(see
.B TYPICAL USAGE
step 3).
A dedicated MGS-only node does not process the
.B params
log, so
.B -P
does not restore the persisted value there.
.PP
For a dedicated MGS-only node (which does not process the
.B params
log), set the
.B mgs
kernel module's boot-time
.B allow_register
module parameter in
.B modprobe.d
for enforcement:
.EX
.B options mgs allow_register=0
.EE
in a
.B modprobe.d
configuration file (see
.BR modprobe.d (5)).
This seeds the
.B obdclass
global at module load time and ensures the policy is reliably re-applied
on every boot.
The module parameter accepts the same
.BR 0 ,
.BR unlimited ,
and
.IR TIMEOUT [ UNITS ]
syntax as the runtime parameter.
It is consulted only once when the
.B mgs
module loads.
A subsequent
.B lctl set_param allow_register=
.I VALUE
overrides it for the remainder of the module's lifetime.
On a combined MGS/MDT, if both the
.B mgs
module parameter and a persisted
.B -P
value are configured, the module parameter is applied first at module load
time, and the persisted
.B params
log value is applied afterwards when the MDT mounts and replays it, so the
persisted
.B -P
value takes precedence.
.PP
A persisted value always takes priority over a locally-set temporary
window, whenever it is replayed. To avoid losing a window early on a
combined MGS/MDT, register all targets sharing the MGS node first, or
temporarily persist
.B unlimited
instead of
.B 0
for the maintenance window.
.SH TYPICAL USAGE
The intended production workflow combines a persistent lockdown with a
temporary, local override:
.IP 1. 4
Persistently disable registration
.RB ( "lctl set_param -P allow_register=0"
on a combined MGS/MDT, or
.B "options mgs allow_register=0"
in
.B modprobe.d
on a dedicated MGS; see
.B PERSISTENCE
above).
This is the steady-state value the MGS returns to after every reboot.
.IP 2. 4
When a new target needs to be added, open a temporary window directly on
the MGS with a
.I local
(non-persistent)
.BR "lctl set_param allow_register=" N ,
for example
.BR "lctl set_param allow_register=1h" .
This does
.B not
change the persisted
.B 0
value, it only overrides the live, in-memory value on the running MGS.
.IP 3. 4
Register the new target(s)
.RI ( "mkfs.lustre \-\-mgsnode=..." ,
or a target mount that triggers registration) while the window is open.
.B Caution:
on a combined MGS/MDT, mounting another target on the
.B same
node while the window is open can close it early; see
.B PERSISTENCE
above.
.IP 4. 4
The window counts down and registration is automatically disabled again
.RB ( allow_register
reverts to
.BR 0 )
when it expires, with no further action required.
If registration is not needed anymore, it can also be disabled immediately
with
.BR "lctl set_param allow_register=0" .
.PP
Because the temporary window is set locally and is never written to the
.B params
config log (see
.BR PERSISTENCE ),
a node reboot or
.B obdclass
reload comes back up with registration disabled by default, even if
an administrator forgot to close the window manually
(modulo the one-time automatic default window described in
.BR "AUTOMATIC DEFAULT WINDOW" ,
which can only reopen if the MGS has no filesystem config log at all,
i.e. a reformatted or never-used MGS. It does
.B not
reopen for an established MGS that already manages at least one
filesystem, even when asked about a different, still-unknown filesystem name).
On a combined MGS/MDT, a persisted
.B "lctl set_param -P allow_register=0"
is reapplied on reboot before any target can register, reinforcing the
lockdown. On a dedicated MGS-only node,
.B -P
is not processed (see
.BR PERSISTENCE ),
so the
.B mgs
module's boot-time parameter is the reliable way to enforce lockdown
across reloads.
Note that simply stopping and starting the MGS service, without an
.B obdclass
reload, does
.B not
reset
.B allow_register
at all (see
.BR PERSISTENCE ):
whatever value was live before the restart, including an open temporary
window, remains in effect afterward.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Setting
.B allow_register
to a time-limited value or 0 improves cluster security by preventing
unauthorized targets from registering with the MGS.
When new targets need to be added to a running cluster, a non-zero
duration needs to be set, with enough time to register the target.
It is prudent to allow some excess time margin, and then set it
explicitly back to
.B 0
once the maintenance is completed.
.PP
Best practices:
.IP \(bu 2
Set
.B allow_register=0
persistently during normal production operation.
On a combined MGS/MDT use
.BR "lctl set_param -P allow_register=0" ,
on a dedicated MGS set
.B "options mgs allow_register=0"
in
.B modprobe.d
(see
.B PERSISTENCE
above)
.IP \(bu 2
When adding new targets, open a temporary, local (non-persistent) window
with
.B "lctl set_param allow_register=" N
on the MGS rather than changing the persistent setting; see
.B TYPICAL USAGE
above
.IP \(bu 2
Use
.B unlimited
only in development or test environments
.IP \(bu 2
Before running
.B "tunefs.lustre \-\-writeconf"
on any target while
.B allow_register
is
.BR 0 ,
open a registration window first. A
.B \-\-writeconf
erases that filesystem's config logs on the MGS. If registration is
still disabled when the targets restart and try to re-register, they
fail with a database access error and the erased logs are not
regenerated. See
.BR tunefs.lustre (8)
.SH MODULES
This parameter is available on the MGS in the following modules:
.EX
.B obdclass
.EE
.PP
The boot-time module parameter of the same name is provided by the
.B mgs
module:
.EX
.B mgs
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
Disable registration immediately:
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param allow_register=0
.EE
.PP
Enable registration for 60 seconds:
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param allow_register=60s
.EE
.PP
Enable registration for 1 hour:
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param allow_register=1h
.EE
.PP
Enable registration indefinitely:
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param allow_register=unlimited
.EE
.PP
Check current registration status:
.EX
.B mgs# lctl get_param allow_register
.EE
.PP
Persist lockdown across reboot (combined MGS/MDT, see
.BR PERSISTENCE ):
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param -P allow_register=0
.EE
.PP
Lock down registration at boot on a dedicated MGS-only node
(in a
.B modprobe.d
configuration file):
.EX
.B options mgs allow_register=0
.EE
.PP
Typical production sequence: persistent lockdown, temporary local window
to add a target, then automatic re-lockdown (see
.BR "TYPICAL USAGE" ):
.EX
.B mgs# lctl set_param -P allow_register=0
.B mgs# lctl set_param allow_register=1h
.B mgs# lctl get_param allow_register
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B allow_register
is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
filesystem package since release 2.18.0.
